Z||000013||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||03:01 AM||||First off, Im a Fiero nurse compared to Keith aka Fiero Doc and to the knowledge and engineering skills of John Parenti. I learned from my experiences with my Fieros and Fiero I have worked on or assisted with at Fiero Motorsports. There have been many threads on this site that I have absolutely no idea of what the solution might be. 84-86 headlights fall into this catagory sorry. I have an 85 GT that I modified with 87 headlights. Honestly, I don't even remember how I did it.<P>As for the EGR blocking plan. Im not sure what you mean by the manifold end. Yes it is possible to block off the EGR opening without taking off the upper plenum. I beleive they are 10MM bolts. Is it easy?  For the most part yes. You might want to take off the dist. cap for some more room. Those coolant tubes can be a real problem too. I took those off of mine during my rebuild.<BR>And the ajar light wouldn't hamper your headlights. Start with the easy stuff. Put a folded rag on the switch for the hood. If its still coming on try the trunklid switch. Those two are the easiest to illiminate as problems. Good luck!<P>For pictures of Keith's Fiero and John Parenti's car (and others) go to: <A HREF="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/photos.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/photos.htm</A> <BR>Be advised! The Fiero Motorsports link was a proposal for FM. It was rejected. The website will never be updated. It is just adrift in cyberspace...||||reg||2
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||03:13 AM||||Also, the Northstar goes slowly on. Since it is a "side project" it only gets attention when Keith gets some time to work on it. Lately that just has not been the case!||||reg||10
Z||000015||Carrolles||02-04-2000||01:00 PM||||I replaced the EGR tube on my 85SE v-6 4spd without taking anything else off.  It is a bit tight getting to the bolts on the underside of the intake plenum but I did it with a box end wrench.  I don't remember the size but 10mm sounds about right.<P>If the EGR tube is cracked, simply blocking it off at the crossover pipe will not solve a high idle problem since the leak would be between the block off plate and the intake plenum.<P>Try this to see if you have a vacuum leak at the EGR tube.  With the engine running spray some WD-40 on the tube and in the space between the tube and the insulating jacket.  If it is leaking it will pull the WD-40 into the intake and cause the idle to rise.  You can also try wiggling the tube to see if it has an effect on the idle.  Mine would jump around a lot if I wiggled the tube.  When I got the old tube out I stripped off the insulating jacket and found the tube cracked about in half.  It is a corrugated SS tube that will eventually crack due to thermal stress and movement.<P>The Coolant tubes going to/from the throttle body actually supply heat to the throttle body to keep it from icing up on the inside due to refrigeration effect of pressure changes in the throat.  The throttle body could freexe up in the right conditions such as very humid cool day and lots of wide open throttle hard acceleration.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000016||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||01:11 PM||||If the block of plate is put in securely it can block off the hole without leaks. We have done this to get people back on the road during road trips. It is a temp fix at the very least but can bring the idle back down. If it isn't the problem Carroll's solution to find the leak is the best.<P>My apologies, I should have prefaced my statement about the coolant tubes by mentioning that I live in the land without seasons Southern California so I don't have problems with the throttle freezing up.||||reg||1
